Chapter: RocEarlyReturnPredicate
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 Ported from roc-lang/roc, src/eval/test/eval_closure_recursion_tests.zig,
 "inspect: early return in for loop predicate function":

     {
         f = |list| {
             for item in list {
                 if item == 2 {
                     return True
                 }
             }
             False
         }
         f([1, 2, 3])
     }

 and Roc expects True.

 One adaptation, and it is the loop again: Codex has no early `return` and
 no imperative `for`, so the scan is a named recursive definition whose
 base case is the end of the list and whose hit case answers without
 recursing further. That is the same control flow -- stop at the first
 match, do not look at the rest -- expressed the only way Codex expresses
 it.

 **What survives is the property the case is named for**: the scan must
 STOP. The list is `[1, 2, 3]` and the match is in the middle, so a scan
 that runs to the end still answers True and looks correct. What separates
 them is that the third element is never read, which this port cannot
 observe from the answer alone -- so the value here is the shape reaching
 the emitter, not a discriminating answer. Recorded rather than papered
 over: a stronger version of this case wants a list whose tail would fault
 or diverge, and that is a different port.

 The Boolean is rendered with `show`, and if Codex spells the tag
 differently from Roc's `True` the difference is in the renderer and is
 recorded in the `.expected` beside this file.

Section: Functions

  scan-for-two : List Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  scan-for-two (xs) (i) =
   if i >= list-length xs then False
   else if list-at xs i == 2 then True
   else scan-for-two xs (i + 1)

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing
  opening = act
   print-line-uni (show (scan-for-two [1, 2, 3] 0))
  end
